# Contact form configuration — copy this file to `.env` and fill it in.
#
# `.env` is git-ignored, so it never reaches the public repository.
#
# Where to get the key:
#   1. Go to https://web3forms.com and create a free account.
#   2. Dashboard → Access Keys → copy your key.
#   3. Add the destination email address THERE, on Web3Forms.
#
# The studio email is deliberately NOT part of this file. Web3Forms forwards
# submissions to the address registered with your key, so the address never has
# to exist anywhere in the site or in git.
#
# For CI (GitHub Pages deploy), add the same value as a repository secret named
# WEB3FORMS_KEY — the workflow forwards it as PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY.
#
# The key is public by design: it identifies the form, it cannot read the inbox.
# Leave it empty and the contact page falls back to showing Telegram instead of
# a form that would post nowhere.

PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALERTA: guardá este archivo en UTF-8 SIN BOM.
#
# Si el archivo arranca con los bytes EF BB BF, la variable se llama
# "<BOM>PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY", Astro no la encuentra, y la web muestra el aviso
# de "formulario no disponible" sin dar error. Si te pasa: abrilo en VS Code,
# abajo a la derecha debería decir "UTF-8" — si dice "UTF-8 with BOM",
# clickealo y elegí "Save with encoding → UTF-8" (sin BOM).
#
# Comprobación rápida (PowerShell):
#   (Get-Content .env -Raw).Trim() -match '^PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY=.+'
# True = bien. False = revisá el BOM o que la línea no esté vacía.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------